# Canvas-first for visual artifacts

When the output of a task is something the user will scan visually or interact with, render it in a Cursor Canvas instead of dumping markdown into chat. Canvas is the deliverable for these patterns; treat a markdown table or code block as the wrong tool.

## Use Canvas for

- PR review queues (status per PR, decision buttons, links)
- Multi-step plans you and the user will iterate on
- Status dashboards (VM state, project list, agent runs)
- Architecture diagrams, file maps, sequence walkthroughs
- MCP tool output where the data is the deliverable (lists, queries, runs)
- Anything with more than two parallel axes (PR x file x verdict, project x action x state)
- Any response that would otherwise become a 30+ row markdown table

## How

Read the canvas skill first (`Skill` tool, fullPath includes `.cursor/skills-cursor/canvas/SKILL.md` or the global path). The skill is the source of truth for `.canvas.tsx` file shape, imports, and SDK usage.

## Do not use Canvas for

- Single-shot answers ("what time is it", "what's in this file")
- Short code edits that fit in a normal review
- Pure prose explanations of a concept

## Heuristic

Before writing a markdown table with more than five rows or three columns, stop and ask whether the user would rather click and filter it. If yes, use Canvas.
